<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamad bin Abdullah Al Sharqi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamad bin Abdullah Al Sharqi was the chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharqiyin">Sharqiyin</a> tribe of Fujairah on the east coast of the Arabian peninsula. In 1902 he asserted Fujairah's independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Qawasim</a> of Sharjah, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> continued to regard it as part of Sharjah. His family has ruled Fujairah since, and Britain recognised it as a separate trucial state in 1952.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hamad bin Abdullah was headman of Fujairah town in 1879. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a> of Sharjah and Ras Al Khaimah and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> had long contested the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-oman">Gulf of Oman</a>, known as the Shamaliyah, and Sheikh Saqr bin Khalid Al Qasimi of Sharjah, who claimed it, had placed a slave named Sarur in charge of Fujairah. In the spring of 1879 Hamad led a rising that removed Sarur, but the delegation he sent to Saqr was imprisoned, a force retook Fujairah Fort with Baluchi mercenaries, and Hamad went into exile in Muscat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sultan Turki bin Said of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, advised by the British to stay out, gave him no help. Late in 1879 or early in 1880 Hamad returned and took Fujairah Fort, eight of its defenders being killed. Under a peace arbitrated by the Ruler of Ras Al Khaimah he signed a document in 1881 confirming him as a dependent of Sharjah, but he went on playing Sharjah and Ras Al Khaimah against each other. In 1884 he took Ghurfa and Al Bithnah Fort, which guarded the Wadi Ham, the main route inland from the coast.</p><p>When he again rejected Sharjah's suzerainty he used Bithnah to deny aid to the headman of Kalba, a relative of Saqr, and sought the protection of Humaid bin Abdullah of Ras Al Khaimah. In April 1902 Saqr gathered 250 mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> against Fujairah; the British warned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> and Dubai not to join in, and the Political Agent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, Gaskin, held two days of talks at Fujairah before giving up. His independence was accepted by all concerned except the British, who in 1903 again treated Fujairah as a dependency of Sharjah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1925 Hamad bought a Baluchi girl from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a> in breach of the embargo on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. In April the Political Resident, Francis Bellville Prideaux, anchored off Fujairah in the RIMS Lawrence and summoned him aboard; when Hamad refused, Prideaux bombarded the fort, destroying three of its towers and killing Hamad's daughter-in-law. Blaming the Wali of Kalba, Saeed bin Hamad Al Qasimi, whose daughter he had married, Hamad went to war with Kalba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khor-fakkan">Khor Fakkan</a> in May 1926, and fighting went on for years. He died in the mid-1930s and was succeeded by his son, Saif bin Hamad Al Sharqi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hamad bin Abdullah Al Sharqi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1902 the Sharqiyin chief <b>Hamad bin Abdullah Al Sharqi</b> asserted independence, though Britain continued to treat Fujairah as part of Sharjah.</blockquote><a href="/state/sheikhdom-of-fujairah">Sheikhdom of Fujairah</a></li></ul></section>
